`DocumentCache` (`src/document-cache.ts`, implementing `IDocumentCache`) is the layer between React components and the reactor, and its internal state explains its behaviour:

```ts
private documents = new Map<string, Promise<PHDocument>>();
private batchPromises = new Map<string, {
  promises: Promise<PHDocument>[];
  promise: Promise<PHDocument[]>;
}>();
private listeners = new Map<string, (() => void)[]>();
```

Three responsibilities, one per map. **Document promise caching** stores the *promise* rather than the resolved document, which is what deduplicates concurrent fetches for the same id — two components asking at once share one round trip. **Batch promise caching** keeps a stable object for `useDocuments`, which matters because a new array identity on every render would re-suspend the component forever. **Subscription management** tracks listeners per document, and the cache subscribes to reactor events so cached data updates automatically rather than on a poll.

Caching promises is also what makes React Suspense integration work at all: a hook throws the pending promise, React re-renders on resolution, and the second render finds the settled promise in the same map. It is the reason a "not-yet" rejection must never be cached — a rejected promise in that map poisons every later render of the same id.

